          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been having trouble sleeping for about three months.",
          "answer": "Chronic sleep difficulties, also known as chronic insomnia disorder, involve trouble falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking too early. This typically occurs at least three nights a week for at least three months, even when there's enough opportunity to sleep, and it leads to daytime issues like fatigue, difficulty concentrating, or mood changes.\n\nSeveral factors can contribute to chronic sleep difficulties. These include other medical conditions like obstructive sleep apnea, restless legs syndrome, chronic pain, depression, and anxiety. Lifestyle factors such as shift work, caffeine, alcohol, nicotine, stimulants, and certain medications can also play a role.\n\nWhen assessing sleep difficulties, a clinician might suggest keeping a sleep diary for one to two weeks. They would also look for underlying conditions and review your use of caffeine, alcohol, nicotine, and your work schedule.\n\nA general approach to managing sleep difficulties includes maintaining a consistent wake-up time every day, even on weekends. This helps regulate your body clock. While bedtime can be flexible, keeping your wake-up time steady is considered the most helpful anchor for sleep. These are supportive measures, and a clinician can help determine the best approach for your specific situation.\n\n(Sources: `guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line`, `condition-insomnia`, `lifestyle-insomnia-sleep`)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
