          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is medication the usual answer?",
          "answer": "Medication is not always the first or only answer for chronic sleep difficulties. For chronic insomnia, both sleep medicine and internal medicine guidelines recommend cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) as the first-line treatment, ahead of medication.\n\nThe benefits of CBT-I can last after treatment ends, unlike pharmacological effects, which generally do not. It also helps avoid the potential harms associated with long-term use of sleep medications. When medication is used, it is typically considered a second-line option and is time-limited.\n\nThe most suitable treatment depends on the specific type of sleep difficulty, its underlying causes, and individual circumstances. For example, if another medical condition like sleep apnea or restless legs syndrome is causing the sleep problem, treating that condition would be the primary approach. Lifestyle factors also play a significant role.\n\n(Sources: `guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line`, `condition-insomnia`)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
